.Dd November 9, 2026
.Dt SCHED_MIC 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sched_mic
.Nd hybrid-core-aware scheduler
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "options SCHED_MIC"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
scheduler, also known as Michaelangelo, is derived from
.Xr sched_ule 4
and retains all of its behavior, adding awareness of heterogeneous
.Pq Dq hybrid
CPU cores on x86 systems.
On processors that mix fast and efficient cores it weighs four core
priority classes when placing a thread, preferring, in order:
.Pp
.Bl -enum -compact -offset indent
.It
performance cores
.Pq Intel P-cores, AMD 3D V-Cache CCD cores ;
.It
efficiency cores
.Pq Intel E-cores, AMD compute-CCD cores, AMD mobile \(dqC\(dq cores ;
.It
the second SMT thread of an already busy core; and
.It
Intel low-power
.Pq LP-E
cores, which are used last.
.El
.Pp
The preference is a soft, tunable bias: real load always decides first,
and the class only orders CPUs whose load is otherwise acceptable.
It is applied when selecting a CPU for a thread, when the long-term load
balancer chooses where to move a thread, and when an idle CPU looks for
work to steal.
Only queued threads can be stolen, never a running one.
An idle CPU of the same or a better class than the CPU holding a queued
thread steals it as soon as
.Va kern.sched.steal_thresh
allows, as in
.Xr sched_ule 4 .
An idle CPU of a worse class, including the idle SMT thread of a core whose
other thread is busy, waits until the thread has been queued for
.Va kern.sched.steal_delay
ticks (about 4 ms by default).
An idle performance core therefore gets a thread queued on another
performance core first, a thread briefly preempted by an interrupt handler
or a daemon simply resumes where it was instead of bouncing to an
efficiency core, and a real backlog on saturated performance cores still
spills to idle efficiency cores after the delay.
When comparing groups of CPUs the best available slot's class-adjusted load
decides first and the group's total load, the
.Xr sched_ule 4
criterion, only breaks ties, so the fill order holds regardless of how many
CPUs share a cache: on an Intel hybrid part with four P-cores and eight
E-cores, four CPU-bound threads occupy one thread of each P-core, eight
occupy the P-cores and four E-cores, twelve the P-cores and all E-cores,
and only then are the second SMT threads of the P-cores used.
On homogeneous hardware, and on architectures other than x86,
.Nm
behaves identically to
.Xr sched_ule 4 .
.Pp
Core class is supplied per-CPU by the
.Xr cpucap 9
capability layer, which the scheduler consults only when a provider commits
new values; the placement and stealing paths read precomputed per-CPU
tables and never query the layer, read model-specific registers or walk the
topology.
On x86 the layer's CPUID provider detects the class at boot:
Intel performance and efficiency cores are distinguished using the
CPUID hybrid core-type leaf;
AMD cache and compute dies are distinguished by comparing per-die
last-level cache sizes.
Processors that are not recognized as hybrid, and symmetric or
single-die parts, are treated entirely as performance cores.
The per-CPU classes, capacities and any dynamic scores are reported under
.Va kern.cpucap .
.Pp
On Intel processors with the Hardware Feedback Interface the
.Xr hfi 4
driver publishes a dynamic performance and efficiency score for every CPU
through the same layer, and the processor may revise them at run time as
thermal and power limits change.
When
.Va kern.sched.use_scores
is set the per-CPU costs are derived from those scores instead of the
fixed class weights, as described under that variable; each revision
flows from the driver through
.Xr cpucap 9
to a recomputation of the tables in thread context, so the hot paths are
the same in both modes.
.Pp
On processors with Intel Thread Director, the per-thread extension of that
interface, the
.Xr hfi 4
driver can also enable the processor's classification of the running
thread by the instructions it executes, and publish a separate score pair
per thread class
.Pq four on current parts
for every CPU.
This is off by default and enabled with the
.Va hw.hfi.itd
loader tunable.
With it on, and
.Va kern.sched.use_scores
and
.Va kern.sched.itd
set,
.Nm
keeps one cost table per thread class, reads the running thread's class
from the processor once per statistics clock tick, and places a thread
using the table of its class, so a thread whose class the processor rates
nearly as well on an efficiency core as on a performance core is steered
less strongly towards the latter than one it rates much lower there.
A thread without a class yet, every thread while Thread Director is off,
and every class the processor does not publish use the plain table, so
the feature off is exactly the behavior described above.
At every context switch the scheduler executes the
.Li HRESET
instruction, when the processor has it, so one thread's history is not
read as the next thread's.
.Pp
Because
.Nm
is derived from
.Xr sched_ule 4 ,
all of that scheduler's
.Va kern.sched
sysctls apply.
The following additional sysctls control hybrid-core placement:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va kern.sched.class_weight_eff
Load penalty added to efficiency-class cores
.Pq class 2 .
.It Va kern.sched.class_weight_lp
Load penalty added to low-power
.Pq LP-E, class 4
cores, scheduled last.
.It Va kern.sched.smt_busy_penalty
Penalty applied to a free thread whose SMT sibling is busy.
Setting this to
.Li 0
restores the SMT placement behavior of
.Xr sched_ule 4 .
.It Va kern.sched.prefer_compute
Selects which of the two main core classes is filled first.
.Li 1
prefers the platform's compute cores:
Intel P-cores, or on AMD parts with asymmetric last-level caches the
smaller-cache compute
.Pq frequency
or
.Dq C
core die.
.Li 0
prefers the other class:
Intel E-cores, or the AMD large-cache
.Pq 3D V-Cache
die.
The default is
.Li 1
except on AMD X3D parts, where it is
.Li 0 .
Low-power
.Pq class 4
cores are always used last.
Which class holds the compute cores is reported by the read-only
.Va kern.sched.compute_class .
.It Va kern.sched.steal_delay
Ticks a thread must have been queued on a CPU before an idle CPU of a worse
class may steal it; see above.
.It Va kern.sched.use_scores
When set to
.Li 1
and a dynamic
.Xr cpucap 9
provider such as
.Xr hfi 4
is active, the cost of each CPU is derived from its score rather than from
.Va kern.sched.class_weight_eff
and
.Va kern.sched.class_weight_lp .
With
.Va s
the CPU's performance score, or its efficiency score when
.Va kern.sched.prefer_compute
is
.Li 0 ,
and
.Va m
the best such score of any CPU, the cost is
.Pp
.Dl "cost = (m - s) * 384 / m"
.Pp
so the best CPUs cost
.Li 0
and every other CPU pays one and a half threads of load
.Pq 384 points at 256 per thread
per unit of relative shortfall.
On an Intel Core i7-1260P, which reports a performance score of 60 for its
P-cores and 34 for its E-cores, the E-cores cost 166, between the default
.Va class_weight_eff
of 160 and the
.Va smt_busy_penalty
of 192, so the fill order described above does not change; a P-core the
processor throttles to a score of 20 costs 256 and is not used while any
other CPU is idle.
With
.Va prefer_compute
set to
.Li 0
the same processor reports efficiency scores of 92 and 100, so the E-cores
cost
.Li 0
and the P-cores 30.
A score of
.Li 0 ,
by which the processor asks that nothing be run on a CPU, costs 4096
.Pq 16 threads :
a very large penalty but never an exclusion, so a thread that may run
nowhere else is still placed there.
Low-power cores keep at least
.Va class_weight_lp ,
since their fabric and cache handicap is not reflected in the score, and
if every CPU reports a score of
.Li 0
the class weights are used.
For stealing, a CPU whose cost is
.Li 0
ranks with the performance cores, a CPU scoring
.Li 0
or costing at least
.Va class_weight_lp
ranks with the low-power cores, and every other CPU ranks with the
efficiency cores, so a throttled P-core and the E-cores steal from each
other at once.
.Pp
The default is
.Li 0 :
on processors that write the table once at boot, such as the i7-1260P,
the scores only reproduce the class ordering, and the behavior on
processors that revise the table at run time has not yet been validated on
hardware.
The default is expected to become
.Li 1
once it has.
It is also a loader tunable, so score mode can be enabled from
.Xr loader.conf 5 .
Whether the costs in effect derive from scores is reported by the
read-only
.Va kern.sched.scores_active ,
and the costs and ranks themselves by
.Va kern.sched.cpu_cost
and
.Va kern.sched.cpu_rank ,
each a list of
.Dq Va cpuid : Ns Va value
pairs.
Scores can be injected for testing with
.Va kern.cpucap.force_scores ,
see
.Xr cpucap 9 .
.It Va kern.sched.itd
When set to
.Li 1 ,
the default, and
.Xr hfi 4
has enabled Intel Thread Director
.Pq Va hw.hfi.itd
and
.Va kern.sched.use_scores
is on, threads are placed with the cost table of their Thread Director
class as described above; otherwise, or when set to
.Li 0 ,
every thread uses the plain table.
The read-only
.Va kern.sched.itd_active
reports the class source in effect
.Pq 0 off, 1 hardware, 2 the test override below ,
.Va kern.sched.itd_classes
how many classes have a table of their own, and
.Va kern.sched.class_cost
every class's per-CPU costs.
.Va kern.sched.itd_reads ,
.Va kern.sched.itd_invalid
and
.Va kern.sched.itd_changes
count class reads on the tick, reads the processor had no valid class for,
and reads that changed a thread's class, and
.Va kern.sched.itd_hist
reports the reads per class.
.It Va kern.sched.mic.itd_force_class
A debugging aid for exercising the per-class tables without Thread
Director: -1, the default, is off; a class number gives every thread that
class on its next tick; and
.Li 8
.Pq Dv CPUCAP_MAX_CLASSES
spreads threads over the published classes by thread id.
Per-class scores can be injected with
.Va kern.cpucap.force_class_scores ,
see
.Xr cpucap 9 .
.It Va kern.sched.core_class
This read-only sysctl reports the class of each CPU as a list of
.Dq Va cpuid : Ns Va class
pairs, where class 1 is performance, 2 efficiency, and 4 low-power.
It is the same information as
.Va kern.cpucap.class .
.It Va kern.cpucap.force_classes
A boot-time tunable for testing.
When set to a comma-separated list of classes such as
.Dq Li 1,1,1,1,2,2,2,2,4,4
the detected class of CPUs 0, 1, ... is replaced by the listed values
(1 performance, 2 efficiency, 4 low-power) and each such CPU takes the
nominal capacity of its forced class; CPUs beyond the end of the list keep
their detected class.
This allows the hybrid placement paths to be exercised on homogeneous
hardware or in a virtual machine.
A malformed list is ignored with a console message.
The older name
.Va kern.sched.mic.force_classes
is still accepted.
.It Va kern.cpucap.detect_lpe
A boot-time tunable that enables detection of Intel LP-E cores as class 4.
Setting it to
.Li 0
leaves those cores in the efficiency class.
The older name
.Va kern.sched.detect_lpe
is still accepted.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr hfi 4 ,
.Xr sched_4bsd 4 ,
.Xr sched_ule 4 ,
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
.Xr cpucap 9
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
scheduler first appeared in
.Mx 4.0 .
It was named after Michaelangelo the cat.
.Sh AUTHORS
